  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Quiet Treatment Room",
    "overview": "Every screen should feel like walking into a good treatment room: warm, clean, softly lit, nothing out of place, and everything you need within reach. The cream canvas is the room; warm-white cards are the linen laid on top; muted brown is the polished teak you touch to act. Nothing shouts. Hierarchy comes from whitespace and type weight before color.\n\nThe same room serves two very different visitors. Customers on the booking site move slowly and want reassurance: a full-bleed photo, a serif wordmark, generous space, and a booking path with no surprises. Staff at the front desk move fast with a customer waiting: the same materials, but denser, with large touch targets, obvious next actions, and money always legible. Components are soft and composed at rest, then crisp and confident the moment someone has to act.\n\nThe system rejects cold gray, pure black, neon, saturated or purple-blue gradients, glassmorphism, heavy shadows, and dark brown as a large background.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One continuous cream canvas; content sits on warm-white cards with a beige hairline and a barely-there shadow.",
      "Muted brown is the only action color; status colors appear only with a text label.",
      "Thai-first typography in DM Sans + Noto Sans Thai; Cormorant Garamond only for the customer wordmark and hero.",
      "Tabular numbers for every price, time, count, and queue number.",
      "Large, forgiving controls (44px on touch, 40px on desktop)."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The 60-30-10 Rule",
        "body": "Cream and warm-white make up about 60% of any screen, beige fills, borders, and text about 30%, and brown plus status colors at most 10%, only where attention is needed.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Graphics-Only Rule",
        "body": "Sandalwood (#a9825e) and the bright status fills (#6f9277, #c87870) never carry text; text uses the matching ink (#557560, #9e5149, #72543e).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Never-Color-Alone Rule",
        "body": "Every status shown by color also has a word or icon (badge with label, dot plus text).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Money Rule",
        "body": "Prices, times, dates, counts, and queue numbers use tabular figures (`.num`) so columns of money line up.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The 13px Floor Rule",
        "body": "Readable text never goes below 13px; 12px is reserved for chart ticks, timelines, and bottom-nav labels, and text is never dimmed with opacity to look secondary (use Driftwood instead).",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Serif Rule",
        "body": "Cormorant Garamond appears only on the customer site's wordmark and hero; the staff app is sans throughout.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Border-Before-Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Prefer a hairline beige border over a shadow; never stack heavy shadows or add one to every card in a list.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep the canvas Cream Canvas (#f8f3ed) everywhere and put content on Warm Linen (#fffcf8) cards with a Hairline Beige border.",
      "**Do** use Teak Brown (#93704f) only for the primary action on a view and for active/selected states.",
